error[cell_cannot_end]: `consumes` on `@a` of `g5_free` says the call ends the life of the `G5` it takes in, and `@` says the opposite: `a` is a cell `g5_free` writes into, where the `G5` comes out to the program, and a cell that receives a handle cannot end one
  at tests/golden/full/fixedbugs-2242-where-the-group-marks-the-ending-parameter-the-note-leaves-the-call-alone.hero:13:23
     |
  13 |     function g5_free(@a: G5 consumes)  #~ cell_cannot_end
     |                       ^
  note: say how the handle the cell receives begins, with a mark on the cell: `acquires <releaser>` if the program owes its release, or `borrows` if the library keeps it. If `g5_free` really takes the handle in, drop the `@`: a parameter written without it hands the value to C, and `consumes` reads there

error[consumed_borrowed_handle]: `g5_free` ends the life of what it is given, and `h` was borrowed — this function promised not to change it
  at tests/golden/full/fixedbugs-2242-where-the-group-marks-the-ending-parameter-the-note-leaves-the-call-alone.hero:16:13
     |
  16 |     g5_free(h)  #~ consumed_borrowed_handle marker_mismatch
     |             ^
  note: a parameter written without `@` is borrowed: the caller keeps the value and reads it again after the call, so a call that frees it leaves them holding an address C has taken back. Write the parameter `@h` in the signature of `gone` and `@` before its argument at every call of `gone`, which says on both lines that the value does not survive
  note: this refuses the call, not the copy: a copy made BEFORE the call still holds the freed address, and nothing here sees that. design.md Part 8 wart 20 states that cost and names what would close it

error[marker_mismatch]: `g5_free` changes this argument, so the call site writes `@` too
  at tests/golden/full/fixedbugs-2242-where-the-group-marks-the-ending-parameter-the-note-leaves-the-call-alone.hero:16:13
     |
  16 |     g5_free(h)  #~ consumed_borrowed_handle marker_mismatch
     |             ^
  note: `h` is a parameter without `@`, so `@h` would be refused: mark it `@h` in the signature too, and at every call
